  "entries": {
    "buff": {
      "word": "BUFF",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A sort of leather, prepared from the skin of the buffalo, dressed with oil, like chamois; also, the skins of oxen, elks, and other animals, dressed in like manner. \"A suit of buff.\" Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The color to buff; a light yellow, shading toward pink, gray, or brown. A visage rough, Deformed, unfeatured, and a skin of buff. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A military coat, made of buff leather. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Med.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The grayish viscid substance constituting the buffy coat. See Buffy coat, under Buffy, a.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mech.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A wheel covered with buff leather, and used in polishing cutlery, spoons, etc.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The bare skin; as, to strip to the buff. [Colloq.] To be in buff is equivalent to being naked. Wright.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Made of buff leather. Goldsmith.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "Of the color of buff. Buff coat, a close, military outer garment, with short sleeves, and laced tightly over the chest, made of buffalo skin, or other thick and elastic material, worn by soldiers in the 17th century as a defensive covering. cloth of a buff color. [Obs.] Nares. used in polishing.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "To polish with a buff. See Buff, n., 5.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To strike. [Obs.] B. Jonson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A buffet; a blow; -- obsolete except in the phrase \"Blindman's buff.\" Nathless so sore a buff to him it lent That made him reel. Spenser.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Firm; sturdy. And for the good old cause stood buff, 'Gainst many a bitter kick and cuff. Hudibras.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        }
      ],
